RangeForce: Stealing NTLM Hashes With Responder
Platform: RangeForce
Room: Stealing NTLM Hashes With Responder
Difficulty: Expert
Date: 2026
Objective
The objective of this lab was to capture and exploit NTLM authentication hashes using network-based attacks. The focus was on leveraging Responder to intercept authentication attempts, understanding differences between NTLM and NTLMv2, and cracking captured hashes to gain unauthorized access.
Step 1: Capturing NTLM Hashes with Responder
I began by using Responder to listen for and intercept authentication requests on the network.
During this process, I was able to:
Capture NTLM/NTLMv2 authentication hashes
Observe authentication attempts from target systems
This demonstrated how attackers can exploit network protocols to capture credential material without directly interacting with the victim.
Step 2: Understanding NTLM vs NTLMv2
Next, I analyzed the differences between NTLM and NTLMv2 authentication mechanisms.
Key observations included:
NTLMv2 provides improved security over NTLM
Both mechanisms still rely on hashed credentials that can be intercepted
This highlighted that even improved authentication protocols can still be targeted if not properly secured.
Step 3: Cracking the Captured Hashes
With the hashes captured, I used John the Ripper to attempt password cracking.
This allowed:
Conversion of captured hashes into crackable format
Successful recovery of plaintext credentials
This step showed how weak passwords can be quickly compromised once hashes are obtained.
Step 4: Gaining Access Using Cracked Credentials
After recovering the plaintext password, I used the credentials to authenticate to the target system.
This resulted in:
Successful login to the system
Validation of the compromised credentials
This confirmed that captured and cracked hashes can directly lead to unauthorized access.
Step 5: Impact Analysis
Combining all findings, the environment was vulnerable due to multiple weaknesses:
Susceptibility to LLMNR/NBT-NS poisoning attacks
Exposure of NTLM/NTLMv2 hashes over the network
Weak password policies allowing successful cracking
An attacker could leverage these issues to:
Capture authentication hashes passively
Crack passwords offline
Gain unauthorized access to systems
Move laterally within the network
Conclusion & Outcome
Through this lab, I gained hands-on experience capturing and exploiting NTLM authentication using Responder. This exercise reinforced the importance of disabling insecure network protocols, enforcing strong password policies, and protecting authentication mechanisms.
It also highlighted how network-based attacks combined with weak credentials can lead to full system compromise.
